Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python script with the following specification: Create a standalone Python 3
command-line script, intended to run under Termux on Android (use the shebang
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`).
Its purpose is to scan the current working directory recursively for Python
source files and report every class, sync function, and async function whose
body consists solely of a single string literal (a docstring-only stub), listing
each with its kind, name, and line number.
Requirements: - Begin the file with the `from __future__ import annotations`
import so that modern type-hint syntax works on older Python versions.
Use only the standard library: `ast`, `pathlib.Path`, and `typing.Iterator`. -
Provide a generator helper that takes a root `Path` and yields every `*.py` file
found recursively via `rglob`, skipping anything that is not a regular file. -
Provide a predicate that takes a function or class AST node (sync function,
async function, or class definition) and returns true only when the node's body
has exactly one statement, that statement is an expression statement, and its
value is a string constant.
Otherwise return false. - Provide a per-file scanner that takes a `Path` and
returns a list of tuples of `(kind, name, line_number)`, where kind is one of
`"class"`, `"async function"`, or `"function"`.
Behavior: - Read the file as UTF-8 text.
If decoding fails or an OS-level read error occurs, silently return an empty
list. - Parse the source with `ast.parse`, passing the file path as the
filename.
If a `SyntaxError` is raised, silently return an empty list. - Walk the entire
AST with `ast.walk` (so nested definitions are included), classify each node by
type (checking `ClassDef`, then `AsyncFunctionDef`, then `FunctionDef`, since
order matters), skip all other node types, and record the finding when the
docstring-only predicate holds, using the node's name and `lineno`. - Provide a
`main()` function with no return value that uses the current working directory
as the root (`Path.cwd()`), iterates over the discovered files, scans each, and
prints the results.
Include a standard `if __name__ == "__main__":` guard calling `main()`.
Output expectations to specify for the agent: print findings in a clear,
human-readable form that includes the relative file path (relative to the root),
the line number, the kind, and the name, and print a summary count at the end.
Print nothing noisy for files that are unreadable or unparsable.
Keep the script free of third-party dependencies, comments limited to what is
useful, and naming in plain snake_case.
The script should be executable directly from the shell and should not require
any arguments. ---
